{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2680 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "197e3b4e3f40159112f99b3fc170b97f9991ca1cffab1f69e27ae430b17a521c" :edb/db-before-t 2679 :edb/db-after-t 2680 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535992 50 #inst "2026-10-15T16:27:46.912Z" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186133893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553771 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186133893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553772 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186133893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553774 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186133893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553777 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186133893 1038 #edb/ref 17592186553794 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186133893 1040 #edb/ref 17592186553804 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553769 1080 "3\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553769 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553769 1083 #uuid "0752d09c-5f6a-5f53-b2ee-4d14923c79a7" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553770 1080 "9\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553770 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553770 1083 #uuid "09332eae-5b2a-514b-8867-2093558d05b2" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553771 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553786 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553771 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553771 1071 "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}9\\left({y}^{2}-2x\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(2,3),$ as shown above." 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553771 1072 #uuid "0da10636-70da-5542-b982-fb7b4f87a3a4" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553771 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(0,1)$ and $(2,3).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(0+t(2-0)),\\,(1+t(3-1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1+2t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=9\\left({y}^{2}-2x\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =9\\left((1+2t)^{2}-2(2t)\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(1+4t+4{t}^{2}-4t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(4{t}^{2}+1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =9\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{t}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2{[\\frac{4{t}^{3}}{3}+t]}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(2(\\frac{4}{3}+1)-2(0)\\right)\\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{14}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553779 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553772 1071 "![](images/ab/ab95b870025708090e40cbd7645072e8f4453cf50047b2a00036a30171c81994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}3\\left(\\sqrt{x-1}+y\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,2)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553772 1072 #uuid "108f6b56-f476-502d-ac1b-82f7c7bee892" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553772 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,2)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3\\left(\\sqrt{x-1}+y\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3\\left(\\sqrt{(1+t)-1}+2-t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left(\\sqrt{t}-t+2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt{t}-t+2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left({\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{1/2}-t+2)\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left({[\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left((\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}+2)-(0)\\right)\\\\  & =3\\left(\\frac{13}{6}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553773 1080 "7\\left(9\\right)" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553773 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553773 1083 #uuid "28779ab3-b956-50e6-818b-e6f98c82ef13" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553800 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553774 1071 "![](images/0e/0e5257867dae67451500793687a2da24ee31a73a17c691d8d8a6dcded46e8993.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}7\\left(x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path along the three-quarters of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=9,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(3,0),$ as shown above." 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553774 1072 #uuid "2b42b22b-83d6-590d-b354-39580363db2b" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553774 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7\\left(x{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\left((3\\operatorname{cos}t){e}^{1-\\operatorname{sin}^{2}t}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(3\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7\\left({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t})\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t,$ $\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\left(-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\operatorname{cos}t{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}^{2}t}\\,\\text{d}t\\right)& =7\\left(-9{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{1}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}[{e}^{1}-{e}^{0}]\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot (e-1)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(\\frac{9}{2}(e-1)\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553775 1080 "7\\left({e}^{2}+1\\right)" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553775 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553775 1083 #uuid "2cbd8d3d-bf94-5248-baf7-04662b6aadb2" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553776 1080 "7\\left(1\\right)" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553776 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553776 1083 #uuid "39e080af-d60a-5141-9d7b-170da955f54f" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553799 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553777 1071 "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}7\\left({x}^{2}y\\right)\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553777 1072 #uuid "3d3f41fe-2ff6-5192-9b2b-6d5b3c48b78f" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553777 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=7\\left({x}^{2}y\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =7\\left((\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7\\left({\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{2}t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\left(4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}t{cos}^{3}t\\,\\text{d}t\\right)& =7\\left(4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,(-\\text{d}u)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-{u}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-[(0)^{4}-(-1)^{4}]\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(-(-1)\\right)\\\\  & =7\\left(1\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553778 1080 "9\\left(\\frac{11}{6}\\right)" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553778 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553778 1083 #uuid "420c6f0f-9dce-576e-9b0d-e95c7499ccb6" 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553769 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553780 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553783 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186553787 13194139535992 17592186045443 true] [17592186553779 1075 #edb/ref 1759218